    <content>
      <Value><![CDATA[## Don't Repeat Yourself

When I started building this site, I had three page templates that all needed the same navigation bar and footer. I could have copied the HTML into each template. It would have worked.

But it would have been *wrong*.

## Enter Partial Views

Instead, I created two partial views:

- `_Navigation.cshtml` — the top navigation bar
- `_Footer.cshtml` — the footer with credits

Each template includes them with a single line:

```csharp
@await Html.PartialAsync("_Navigation")
```

## Why This Matters

When I later needed to update the navigation to dynamically list all top-level pages, I changed *one file*. Every page on the site instantly picked up the new nav links. If I'd duplicated the markup, I would have needed to update three files — and risk them getting out of sync.

## The Active State Challenge

One subtle detail: the navigation highlights the current page. On the Blog List page, "Blog" is highlighted. On a blog post, "Blog" is *still* highlighted because the post is a descendant of the Blog List.

This required a small but elegant check:

```csharp
Model.IsDescendantOrSelf(child)
```

Umbraco's content tree makes this trivial — every node knows its ancestors. It's one of those features you don't appreciate until you need it.

## Composition Over Inheritance

Partial views embody the composition pattern: build small, focused components and assemble them into larger structures. It's a principle that applies far beyond web development — it's how good software is built at every scale.]]></Value>
